WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.970
Hi! What do a school bag, water and air have in common? Today we'll learn what matter is, the three states of matter, and how to measure mass and volume.

2
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.738
Matter is anything that has mass and takes up space. A stone, water and air are all matter.

3
00:00:18.888 --> 00:00:26.370
We can't see air, but it is matter. Pump air into a flat football and the ball gets heavier, so air has mass.

4
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:34.146
But light, heat, sound and shadows have no mass and take up no space, so they are not matter.

5
00:00:34.296 --> 00:00:41.202
A solid has a definite shape and a definite volume. A wooden block stays the same wherever you put it.

6
00:00:41.352 --> 00:00:51.546
A liquid has a definite volume but no definite shape. Pour 250 ml of juice into another cup and it is still 250 ml.

7
00:00:51.696 --> 00:01:01.122
A gas has no definite shape and no definite volume. It fills its whole container, and it can be compressed, or squashed.

8
00:01:01.272 --> 00:01:05.418
We measure mass with a balance, in grams or kilograms.

9
00:01:05.568 --> 00:01:15.498
We measure volume with a measuring cylinder, in millilitres. One millilitre is one cubic centimetre. Keep your eye level with the water when you read it.

10
00:01:15.648 --> 00:01:24.282
To find the volume of a stone, read the water first: 50 ml. Put the stone in, and it reads 80 ml.

11
00:01:24.432 --> 00:01:31.794
The water rose by 30 ml, so the stone's volume is 30 cubic centimetres.

12
00:01:31.944 --> 00:01:37.746
Watch out! Many pupils think air isn't matter because we can't see or hold it.

13
00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:48.354
In fact air has mass, and it takes up space. Push an upside-down cup into water and a tissue inside stays dry, because the air in the cup takes up the space.

14
00:01:48.504 --> 00:01:54.090
The things that are not matter are light, heat, sound and shadows.

15
00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:05.154
Your turn! Stone, milk or air: which has a definite volume but no definite shape? Pause and think.

16
00:02:05.304 --> 00:02:14.778
Milk. Milk is a liquid: its volume stays the same, but it takes the shape of its container. Did you get it right?

17
00:02:14.928 --> 00:02:30.018
Another one! A syringe holds 100 ml of air. The nozzle is sealed and the plunger is pushed in to 60 ml. What does this show? Pause and think.

18
00:02:30.168 --> 00:02:40.770
It shows that air can be compressed: it went down by 40 ml. With water inside, the plunger would not move. Did you get it right?

19
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:52.050
Last one! To measure the volume of some cooking oil, which apparatus and which unit should we use? Pause and think.

20
00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:00.306
A measuring cylinder, in millilitres. A balance is for measuring mass. Did you get it right?

21
00:03:00.456 --> 00:03:15.306
Remember: anything with mass that takes up space is matter, even air. A solid has a definite shape and volume, a liquid only a definite volume, and a gas neither. The rise in water level is the volume of the object.

22
00:03:15.456 --> 00:03:21.618
Next time you have a drink, think about which states of matter are in your cup!

